{{also|postdaté}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|post-date}}

===Etymology===
From {{prefix|en|post|date}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpəʊstˌdeɪt/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-postdate.wav|a=Southern England}}
<!--probably 2 prons, heteronym-->

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[occur]] after an [[event]] or [[time]]; to [[exist]] later on in time.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2001|author=David L. Lieber; Jules Harlow|title=Etz Hayim: Torah and Commentary|page=37|text=Because these prophecies of Isaiah '''postdate''' the fall of Babylon (539 B.C.E), they probably refer to that event.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[assign]] an [[effective]] [[date]] to a [[document]] or [[action]] [[later]] than the [[actual]] [[date]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''postdate''' a contract, that is, to date it later than the time when it was in fact made}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To affix a date to after the event. <!--i.e. put the true date on it, but later-->

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|to assign a date later than the actual date}} {{l|en|overdate}}; see also [[Thesaurus:overdate]]

====Antonyms====
* {{antsense|to exist later on in time}} {{l|en|predate}}; see also [[Thesaurus:predate]]
* {{antsense|to assign a date later than the actual date}} {{l|en|predate}}; see also [[Thesaurus:backdate]]

